@@ -0,0 +1,35 @@
+---
+id: dl-005-availability-lists-free-items-of-a-kind-in-id-order
+type: decision-log
+title: "Availability lists free items of a kind in id order"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Context
+
+The desk needs to see at a glance what can still be rented. `quote` deliberately ignores bookings
+(dl-004), so the desk needs a separate query that does look at them. Periods are half-open (dl-001),
+and input is validated strictly at the service boundary (dl-002).
+
+## Decision
+
+`availability({ start, end, kind? })` returns the ids of the equipment that is free for the whole period.
+
+- An item is free when none of its bookings overlaps the period, using the same half-open rule as `book`.
+  A period that starts on the day another ends does not clash with it.
+- `kind` is optional. When given it must be `'board'` or `'sail'` and only items of that kind are
+  considered. When omitted, items of every kind are considered.
+- `start` and `end` are validated as in `quote`: UTC midnights, `end` after `start`. An invalid `kind`
+  throws.
+- The result is an array of ids sorted in ascending order by string comparison, so the same catalogue and
+  bookings always give the same list. An empty array means nothing is free.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The desk can show what is free without first trying to book, and `book` remains the only step that
+  reserves an item. A listed id can still be taken by a booking made after the query.
+- `quote` keeps its behaviour from dl-004: it still prices a period that is not free.
+- The overlap rule is shared by `book` and `availability` in one helper, so the two cannot disagree.
+- Sorting by plain string comparison is deterministic but not natural order: `board-10` comes before
+  `board-2`. A different order would be a new decision.
